{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) #1</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) #2</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/9/129aa112f3e1d4eb1f1ee2b65225adf5d64173dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4567$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> is tripled. The ones digit (units digit) in the resulting number is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/d/b8d3b1a8cbd8ffb12f806333eb8da2a68598d998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 5 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 6 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 7 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 3 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"388\" height=\"18\" />  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Tripling a number means that we are multiplying the number by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We can multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/c/cecb520e1d92febe9ae7ef03477016da9eb1dd25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4567 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"15\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/7/90740029adfeacac5e6f8161eb27323baf090f78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13701$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />. The units digit is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/f/98f841f41e69125694ae219321078c51ebe92b53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"56\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher and original by coolmath34\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can notice that only the units digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/9/129aa112f3e1d4eb1f1ee2b65225adf5d64173dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4567$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> matters here, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/8/24855e602c7133a6233f82381887f23710f66c78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4000 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/9/a490171d8a9e1f3b6c6f8007553651456bd86456.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/b/60b5ddd6fe3490651165ef1aec0e5d6a0588af14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> all have units digits of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, the units digit would be the units digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/7/297bc656c757323326872d4f1a92f86ffcb8d4c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f25d31faf472ddb4c698a14522fe8125c06f8dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/f/98f841f41e69125694ae219321078c51ebe92b53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"56\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher and original by coolmath34\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" 